== Reward Shaping and Proxy Reward Misalignment

The theoretical foundations of reward shaping in reinforcement learning trace back to the seminal work of Ng et al. @ng1999reward, who proved that only _potential-based_ reward shaping preserves the set of optimal policies under the original MDP. Departures from this constraint produce degenerate behaviors, as demonstrated by the canonical bicycle balancing task of Randlov and Alstrom @randlov1998bicycle, where a naive shaping reward incentivized the agent to ride in circles accumulating intermediate reward rather than reaching the goal. Wiewiora et al. @wiewiora2003pbrs established a formal equivalence between potential-based reward shaping and Q-value initialization, providing a unified view of how prior knowledge can be incorporated without distorting the optimal policy. Devlin and Kudenko @devlin2012dynamic extended this framework to dynamic, time-dependent and state-dependent potentials, showing that optimality guarantees are preserved even when the shaping function evolves during training. These results collectively delineate a narrow corridor within which auxiliary rewards remain safe. Any shaping signal that falls outside the potential-based class risks inducing policies that optimize the shaped objective at the expense of the true one.

The broader phenomenon of proxy objectives diverging from true objectives has been formalized under several complementary frameworks. Amodei et al. @amodei2016concrete identified _reward hacking_ as a core challenge in AI safety, where agents discover unintended strategies that satisfy the specified reward without fulfilling the designer's intent. Manheim and Garrabrant @manheim2018goodhart categorized this failure mode through the lens of Goodhart's Law, distinguishing regressional, extremal, causal, and adversarial variants that each describe a different mechanism by which optimizing a proxy measure ceases to improve the true objective. Skalse et al. @skalse2022reward provided formal definitions of reward hacking and proved that it is _inevitable_ whenever the proxy reward and the true reward are not ordinally equivalent, meaning that no amount of careful tuning can eliminate the risk if the reward functions disagree on the ranking of any two policies. Gao et al. @gao2023scaling demonstrated this phenomenon empirically through scaling laws for reward model overoptimization, revealing a characteristic inverted-U relationship in which true reward initially rises as proxy optimization increases but then declines sharply once the policy begins exploiting the proxy. Pan et al. @pan2022reward further showed that reward misspecification produces _predictable_ failure patterns, with agents systematically converging to high-proxy, low-true-reward regions of policy space.

These theoretical and empirical results directly inform our analysis. The IoU-based reward used to train SAM prompt placement policies is precisely a proxy for segmentation quality. It correlates with true mask quality under moderate optimization pressure but, as our experiments demonstrate, eventually induces policy collapse. The agent learns prompt placements that inflate the IoU score without improving, and sometimes degrading, the perceptual quality of the resulting segmentation. This behavior aligns with the inverted-U scaling law of Gao et al. @gao2023scaling and constitutes a concrete instance of the extremal Goodhart failure described by Manheim and Garrabrant @manheim2018goodhart, where the proxy breaks down at the distributional tails that aggressive optimization reaches.
